{
  "description": "Mapping of technology keywords to instruction files for auto-loading context-aware instructions",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "excluded_files_note": "instructions/agents.instructions.md is intentionally NOT referenced anywhere in this file. It's the platform 'core' template (scripts/configs/platform.json → mapping.core.value), copied per chosen AI platform as CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, .cursor/rules/plaesy.mdc, etc. by `plaesy init`'s platform-specific setup (see scripts/internal/scaffold/platform.go:SetupPlatformConfig) — not by the generic instructions copy or `plaesy stack detect` selection this file drives. platform.json's mapping.instructions.excludes already excludes it from the generic copy for the same reason. Do not add it to 'always_load' or any category here.",
  "field_semantics_note": "Verified against scripts/internal/detectstack/detectstack.go (Go port, single cross-platform implementation replacing the old scripts/bash/detect-stack.sh + scripts/powershell/detect-stack.ps1 pair as of 2026-09): only 'file', 'keywords', 'extensions', and 'filenames' are actually read to decide what loads. 'extensions' matches any file with that suffix anywhere under the target (existence-only, e.g. '.docx'); 'filenames' matches an exact filename anywhere under the target (existence-only, e.g. 'next.config.js') — use 'filenames' instead of a keyword when the only reliable signal is a specific config file's name, not its content (a bare dependency name like \"next\" is too generic/prose-colliding to add as a keyword). Do not put literal double-quote characters inside a 'keywords' entry unless you have re-checked detect-stack.go's keyword extraction handles escaping the way you expect. 'patterns' and 'triggers' (per entry) and this file's top-level 'description'/'version' plus 'detection_rules.priority'/'case_insensitive'/'deduplication' are NOT read by the Go implementation — they are documentation/intent only. 'case_insensitive' and 'deduplication' happen to already match real behavior (matching is case-insensitive and output is deduplicated) but are not switches — flipping them here changes nothing. Do not assume editing 'patterns', 'triggers', or 'priority' affects detection; if you need pattern- or trigger-based matching to actually run, it must be implemented in scripts/internal/detectstack first.",
  "pending_rename_note": "RESOLVED 2026-09-27. The file was misspelled 'software-design-prinsiples.instructions.md'; it is now 'software-design-principles.instructions.md' and BOTH mapping entries are restored below: the 'software-design-principles.instructions.md' scope_load row and the 'software-design-principles' cross_cutting block. Neither the rename nor a further consolidation into the other *-design-principles files is in progress — the file is a standalone general-altitude reference (applyTo '**') and is distinct from the domain-specific siblings.",
  "mappings": {
    "always_load_reinstatement_note": "2026-09-27. A slimming pass moved error-recovery, universal-orchestrator and context-engineering out of always_load into scope_load to keep the base context lean. That is wrong for these three: error-recovery is needed the moment anything fails rather than inside a named scope; universal-orchestrator routes requests that match no scoped command, so an agent that cannot see it in context cannot route to it; and context-engineering is named as a framework anchor by scripts/internal/scaffold/copy_test.go. They are reinstated here. date-system stays in scope_load: it is needed only when a document carries a date, so its absence from the base context costs nothing. If you are slimming always_load again, check reachability, not just how often each file is read.",
  "always_load": [
      "plaesy.instructions.md",
        "error-recovery.instructions.md",
        "universal-orchestrator.instructions.md",
        "context-engineering.instructions.md",
